= Jasmine Tea (茉莉花茶)

_A_ scented green tea _(花茶, huāchá): green tea base (typically Fujian-grown small-leaf_ Camellia sinensis _var._ sinensis_) layered with_ Jasminum sambac _flower buds during the summer flowering season and processed by repeated_ scenting (窨花 yìnhuā) _so that the volatile aroma compounds of the flower are absorbed by the dry leaf. The substance is a_ saṃskāra-class transformation _of green tea, classed in the formal Chinese six-tea-category system under 花茶 (scented tea), a related-but-distinct category from the six basic process-categories (green, white, yellow, oolong, red, dark). Status: Chinese — fully traditional in the late-Ming–Qing scented-tea industry, postdating Lu Yu's_ Chajing _by centuries; the canonical Chinese scented tea, with substantial Fujian-and-Guangxi industrial standing. Ayurveda: not classical (jasmine itself is classical — see jasmine-flowers.typ — but the tea-scenting combination is not). Greco-Roman: not classical. Modern Western: the canonical "Chinese tea" of Western tea-shop experience after green tea._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Botanical components:*
- *Camellia sinensis var. sinensis* — the green tea base; small-leaf cultivar (see green-tea.typ for the full Camellia framework). Fujian's *Fuding* (福鼎) and *Fu'an* (福安) are the canonical jasmine-tea base regions, supplying green-tea base of grades from coarse 烘青 (hōngqīng, baked-green) to premium silver-needle white-tea base for high-end jasmine.
- *_Jasminum sambac_ (L.) Aiton*, family Oleaceae — the *Arabian / Sambac jasmine*; the *only* jasmine species used for traditional Chinese jasmine-tea scenting. Distinct from _J. officinale_ (common / poet's jasmine) and _J. grandiflorum_ (Spanish jasmine, the Indian classical jātī used in perfumery and Hindu ritual). See jasmine-flowers.typ for the full Jasminum framework.

*Chinese:* 茉莉花茶 (mòlìhuāchá), "jasmine-flower tea"; or simply 花茶 (huāchá, "flower tea") when context is unambiguous — *jasmine is the canonical and dominant flower used*; the broader 花茶 category includes 桂花茶 (osmanthus tea), 玫瑰花茶 (rose tea), 玉蘭花茶 (magnolia tea), but jasmine-scented green tea so dominates by volume that 花茶 is most often jasmine.

*茉莉 (mòlì)*: from a Middle-Chinese transliteration of Sanskrit *मल्लिका (mallikā)* — the Sanskrit name for _Jasminum sambac_ — confirming the *Buddhist-period import* of the flower into China. The character pair 茉莉 has no native Chinese semantic content; it is unambiguously phonetic borrowing from Sanskrit via Buddhist transmission, attested from the Tang period onward. The jasmine plant itself reached China by the early medieval period via Buddhist commerce.#footnote[The 茉莉 ← _mallikā_ etymology is standard — see _Hanyu Da Cidian_ (漢語大詞典); the Tang-period botanical introduction is documented in standard Chinese horticultural history (Métailié, _Science and Civilisation in China_, vol. 6.4, _Traditional Botany_, Cambridge 2015; Schafer, _The Golden Peaches of Samarkand_, Berkeley 1963, for Tang-period exotic-plant introductions). The Buddhist-loanword pattern is shared with many South-Asian-origin plant names in Chinese.]

*English:* _Jasmine tea_ — straightforward translation. _Jasmine_ from Latin _iasminum_ from Arabic _yāsamīn_ (ياسمين) from Persian _yāsamīn / yāsmīn_ — see jasmine-flowers.typ for the full etymology.

*Sanskrit:* No traditional jasmine-tea entry. The flower mallikā (मल्लिका) is classical; the tea-scenting combination is post-classical to the South Asian canon (tea itself entered India in the 19th c.).

== Processing and category definition

Jasmine tea is *not* a basic tea-processing category but a *secondary saṃskāra* applied to a finished green-tea base. The canonical sequence:

1. *Base tea preparation* — green tea (occasionally white tea, very rarely oolong or even red) is processed to completion as a normal tea; the base is dry, stable, and ready to receive scent.
2. *Jasmine harvest* — _Jasminum sambac_ buds are picked in the *afternoon* (still closed, with full essential-oil load); they will *open and release fragrance in the evening*.
3. *Layering (窨花 yìnhuā, "scenting")* — the closed jasmine buds are layered with the green tea, typically in a roughly 100 kg tea : 30–40 kg flowers ratio for premium grades; the tea absorbs the released aroma as the buds open through the night. Industrial process controls temperature and humidity precisely.
4. *Sieving (篩花 shāihuā)* — after one round of scenting, the spent flowers are sieved out (good jasmine tea typically *does not contain visible flowers* — the appearance of dried flowers in the final tea is sometimes a sign of marketing emphasis rather than quality; the *aroma* is what matters, not the visible flowers).
5. *Reheating (復火 fùhuǒ)* — gentle drying to remove moisture absorbed from the flowers.
6. *Repeat:* steps 3–5 are repeated for premium grades — *2–5 scenting rounds (一窨 yīyìn, 二窨 èryìn, 三窨 sānyìn, 五窨 wǔyìn)* with fresh flowers each time. The number of scentings is the principal quality marker: 七窨一提 (seven scentings plus one finishing scent with flowers retained) is among the highest grades.

The processing is genuinely *saṃskāra*-class — the substance is *transformed*, the green-tea base acquiring not merely an aroma layered on top but a chemistry modified by *adsorption-and-redistribution* of volatile linalool, benzyl acetate, methyl anthranilate, indole, and other jasmine aroma compounds into the leaf matrix.#footnote[Processing technical detail: standard Chinese-tea-industry literature on huacha production — see Heiss & Heiss, _The Story of Tea_ (Ten Speed Press, 2007), chapter on Chinese tea regions; modern reviews in _Food Chemistry_ on jasmine-tea aroma chemistry. The 窨花 / 篩花 / 復火 cycle is industry-standard.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Scented jasmine tea is post-Bencao-Gangmu (1578) as a distinct named drug; the formal medical placement is by extension from the green-tea entry (see green-tea.typ) modified by the jasmine flower's classical attribution. Jasmine flower 茉莉花 enters the late-Qing and modern Chinese pharmacology as a distinct minor drug._

*Property (性):* The base green tea is cool (涼); the jasmine flower is treated as *warm* (溫) in Chinese pharmacology — *the scenting moderates the green tea's cooling toward neutral (平)*, exactly the saṃskāra-effect that the classical reading would predict. Jasmine tea sits between green and oolong in thermal classification.

*Flavor (味):* Sweet (甘), with subtle bitter (微苦) and a *distinctive floral aromatic (芳香)* — the last is the key sensory and pharmacological marker.

*Channels entered (歸經):* The green-tea channels (Heart, Lung, Stomach, Liver, Kidney) supplemented by the jasmine-flower channels: *Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Liver (肝)* — *jasmine specifically opens Spleen-Stomach and resolves Liver-qi constraint (疏肝)*.

*Actions & indications:*
- *Standard green-tea actions* (apply with reduced intensity): 清熱 (clears heat, mildly), 消食 (disperses food stagnation), 利水 (mild diuretic), 提神 (refreshes spirit).
- *Jasmine-specific contributions:* 理氣解鬱 (regulates qi and resolves stagnation — *especially Liver-qi stagnation with emotional component*); 和中 (harmonizes the middle); 辟穢 (disperses turbid odors / clears foul / mild antimicrobial); 開胃 (opens appetite).
- *Combined functional register:* jasmine tea is the canonical *digestive-and-mood* tea of Chinese functional dietetics — clearing food stagnation, resolving qi constraint, lifting spirits; the daytime tea of women, scholars, and the elderly in classical Chinese tea-culture imagery.
- *Modern Chinese restatement:* anti-inflammatory and antioxidant register supports the digestive-and-mood framing; clinical evidence modest.#footnote[Action list compiled from late-Qing and modern Chinese tea-dietetic literature and from the 茉莉花 entries in modern materia medica handbooks (Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed., includes 茉莉花 in the modern functional-medicine register). The jasmine-tea-as-women's-tea cultural image is well documented in Chinese tea-culture literature.]

*Cautions:* yin-vacuity heat with insomnia (the caffeine plus aromatic warming); blazing fire; heavy Spleen-vacuity-damp; pregnancy (caffeine load).

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_No classical Sanskrit entry for jasmine tea; the placement is reasoned cross-system. Tea is post-classical to South Asia; jasmine (mallikā / jātī, see jasmine-flowers.typ) is fully classical. The combination is post-classical._

*Reasoned rasa:* Tikta (bitter, from the tea base) and Kaṣāya (astringent, from the tea tannins), with Madhura (sweet) as a faint aromatic note from the jasmine. The bitter-astringent dominance is the green-tea profile, *softened* by the floral sweet aromatic.

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (light), Rūkṣa (dry) — but *less rūkṣa than plain green tea* (the floral oils contribute a subtle snigdha note); Sūkṣma (subtle, penetrating — the aromatic property of jasmine, in classical Ayurvedic terms applied to volatile-aromatic drugs).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Śīta (cooling) — but *less cooling than plain green tea*; the warming jasmine moderates the green tea's coolness toward neutral. *Net: mildly cooling.*

*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (pungent) — the green-tea vipāka; the jasmine flower would contribute a madhura modulation.

*Prabhāva:* No classical attribution. *Reasoned commentarial:* the *mood-elevating and qi-resolving* action of jasmine flower (cross-system convergence between Chinese 理氣解鬱 and Ayurvedic _manas-prasādana_) carries into the tea; jasmine's specific affinity for *resolving the kind of subtle mental-emotional stagnation that does not respond to standard digestive carminatives* could be argued at prabhāva level — flagged interpretive.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pacifies Kapha (the tea-base profile); *gentler on Vāta and Pitta than plain green tea* (the warming jasmine and aromatic snigdha-sūkṣma profile moderate the laghu-rūkṣa-śīta of the green tea). Of the green-tea family, jasmine green is the *most Vāta-Pitta-friendly* (after hōjicha — see bancha.typ).

*Reasoned karma:* _Manas-prasādana_ (mind-calming / mood-elevating — the canonical jasmine action, see jasmine-flowers.typ); _hṛdya_ (cardiotonic); _medhya_ (mild — green tea); _dīpana-pācana_ (mild digestive); _ucchvāsa-śuddhi_ (breath-clearing — the aromatic register); _kaphahara_; _mūtrala_; _ruci-vardhana_ (appetite-promoting via the aromatic). In excess: _nidrā-nāśana_ (sleep-destroying), _rasa-kṣaya_, _vāta-prakopa_ — but reduced relative to plain green tea.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S, N, F, M)_

- *(F) Conditional cautions:* same as green tea broadly (empty stomach, late evening, iron-supplement timing); slightly milder.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Caffeine load:* ~20–40 mg per cup of brewed jasmine tea (similar to green tea base it uses); standard caffeine guidance.
  - *Iron-binding tannins*: same as green tea — separate from iron-rich meals.
  - *Sourcing*: jasmine tea is occasionally *flavored* with synthetic jasmine essence rather than authentically scented with whole flowers; the synthetic versions lack the linalool-and-indole-and-benzyl-acetate aromatic complexity. *Buy unflavored, multi-scenting Fujian or Guangxi traditionally-processed jasmine tea* for the genuine pharmacology.
  - *Jasmine allergy*: rare but documented.
  - *Pregnancy*: limit caffeine; the modest jasmine flower content does not raise specific pregnancy concerns beyond the caffeine of the tea base.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Jasmine tea is *not classical in Tibb* as a compound substance — tea entered the Persian-Arabic-Ottoman world only in the 17th–19th c. (see green-tea.typ), and the Fujian jasmine-tea scenting tradition is concurrent. *The two component substances are independently treated in Tibb*: the green-tea base is post-classical (see green-tea.typ), and the *Jasminum sambac* flower (Arabic *yāsamīn*) is fully canonical (see jasmine-flowers.typ — Avicennan Hot-Moist 1st degree, *mufariḥ* / mood-elevating). The combination as a single substance is post-classical Chinese in origin. Reasoned modern Unani placement: the scented green tea sits at *Cool-Neutral and Dry* in the 1st degree (the warming jasmine moderates the cooling green tea toward neutral, the same logic as the Chinese reading); *muqawwī al-rūḥ al-ḥaywānī*, *mufariḥ*, *muḥsin al-hawḍm*, mild *mufattiḥ*. The substance is appropriate to the *mood-elevating + mild-digestive* register, which has clean Tibb framework correspondence even though the compound itself is post-classical. See _jasmine-flowers.typ_ for the substantive Avicennan jasmine pharmacology, and _green-tea.typ_ for the broader tea-and-Tibb framework.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

Jasmine tea is a *daily dietetic medicinal*, not a compound-formula ingredient.

- *清飲茉莉花茶 (qīngyǐn mòlìhuāchá)* — plain brewed jasmine tea; ~3–5 g per pot, ~80–85 °C water, 1–3 minute steep; the canonical preparation.
- *Tasse / glass form* — traditional Chinese jasmine tea is often served in *glass tumblers* showcasing the jasmine and the dancing leaves; service tradition in northern China especially.
- *茉莉花茶配桂花 (mòlìhuāchá pèi guìhuā)* — jasmine tea + osmanthus flower; classical floral pairing.
- *龍珠 (Lóngzhū, "Dragon Pearls")* and *龍鳳 (Lóngfèng, "Dragon Phoenix")* — premium hand-rolled jasmine teas; small spheres of green tea and silver-tip white tea hand-rolled and scented; high-end teahouse offerings.
- *Modern industrial bag-tea jasmine* — convenient but typically lower-grade base and fewer scenting rounds.

=== Ayurveda

No classical Ayurvedic formulas. The modern Indian / Bengali use of jasmine tea is a 20th-c. cosmopolitan adoption.

=== Western / Greco-Roman

No classical tradition. Jasmine tea was among the *first Chinese teas* to gain Western recognition in the 19th c.; British and Russian merchants traded it; it appeared in early-20th-c. American and British tea-shops as the canonical Chinese-experience tea. The modern Chinese-restaurant / dim-sum jasmine-tea pairing in Western markets dates to mid-20th-c. Cantonese diaspora.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

The cultivation, processing, and traditional consumption of jasmine tea cohere in a single climate type — *subtropical-humid southern China* (Fujian, Guangxi, Hunan, Jiangsu, Anhui, parts of Sichuan). The substance is climate-specific to that zone; export consumption is cross-climate transposition. See _climates.typ_ for the canonical climate framework.

*Subtropical-humid (cultivation and core consumption — Fujian, Guangxi, Jiangsu).* Hot wet summers and mild damp winters dominate the Fujian and lower-Yangtze tea regions. In Ayurvedic _ṛtucaryā_ terms (Caraka Sūtrasthāna 6) the year is dominated by an extended *varṣā* (monsoon) and *śarad* (post-monsoon) load on Pitta-Kapha, with a brief winter giving only mild relief; in 中醫 terms (_Su Wen_ ch. 12, 異法方宜論) this is the southeastern register where 濕熱 (damp-heat) and the Spleen are loaded across most of the year. *The traditional constitutional response* is light-aromatic-mildly-cooling preparations that move qi without further damping the spleen — and the *scented green tea is exactly that*: jasmine's warming-aromatic moves the qi (_理氣_) and resolves emotional/digestive stagnation that the damp-heat climate predictably generates, while the green-tea base mildly clears damp-heat without the deeper cooling of a plain green tea (which would over-clear into Spleen vacuity in a perpetually-damp climate). The seasonal timing of jasmine flowering (high summer, when damp-heat is at maximum) and tea scenting (immediately following the spring tea harvest) follows the classical _ṛtu-saṃyoga_ — the right preparation is made at the right season. *Cares:* brewed at *cooler water* (~80–85 °C, not boiling) so the aromatic terpenes are not driven off and the green-tea bitterness not fully extracted; served *warm-not-hot* in the southern Chinese tea-room tradition; the floral-aromatic register makes it the appropriate *daytime / midmorning / midafternoon* tea, not the heavy postprandial tea (oolong or red tea fill that role in southern Chinese tea-culture). The classical _saṃyoga_ pairing with *light Cantonese dim-sum* or with *summer cold sweets* (mung bean soup, 涼茶 cooling herbal infusions) makes climatic sense — the tea moves qi alongside the cooling-digestive food preparations.

*Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26).* Jasmine tea exported globally is most-often consumed *iced* in summer (Western specialty-tea and bottled-drink form) and as a *daily-all-climate tea* in homes well outside its native climate; the cool-damp Atlantic European or continental-extreme contexts where jasmine tea reaches modern consumers are *exactly the climates the substance was not made for*. Cool-damp climates carry Vāta-Kapha 寒濕 loads on the Spleen; jasmine tea's mild diuretic-aromatic profile, while not actively harmful, contributes nothing to the constitutional correction those climates need (warming root vegetables, fermented sour preparations, long-cooked fats — see climates.typ Cool-damp temperate). The substance is *fine* in transposition but is *off-key for the climate*; the appropriate response is not to abandon it but to recognize it as the warm-climate guest substance it is and pair it with locally-appropriate accompaniments rather than treat it as a daily-essential. Iced jasmine tea in summer at any climate is climatically appropriate (the kāla-saṃyoga is good); iced jasmine tea in winter in a cool-damp climate is a clear _kāla-viruddha_.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:* the green-tea base (caffeine, catechins led by EGCG, L-theanine, flavonoids — see green-tea.typ) carries the bulk of the *substantive* pharmacology. The jasmine scenting contributes a measurable *aromatic-volatile* fraction:
- *Benzyl acetate* — the principal jasmine aroma compound by mass.
- *Linalool* — also major; well-characterized anxiolytic / mild sedative pharmacology (the lavender-tradition compound).
- *Indole* — distinctive intense floral; in small amounts.
- *Methyl anthranilate, methyl jasmonate, jasmone, eugenol traces, geraniol, nerolidol* — the jasmine volatile spectrum.
- Some of the volatiles are *adsorbed into the leaf matrix* and not lost in storage; some are concentrated in the cell-wall waxes of the tea leaf.#footnote[Jasmine-tea aroma chemistry: reviews in _Food Chemistry_ on huacha aroma; standard analytical literature on _Jasminum sambac_ essential oil — the benzyl acetate / linalool / indole / methyl anthranilate spectrum is well-characterized.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Caffeine + L-theanine + linalool*: the cognitive-calm pharmacology of green tea is *augmented* by the linalool — small clinical evidence for enhanced anxiolytic-and-calm-alert effects of jasmine tea over plain green tea in stress contexts; effect sizes modest.
- *Antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, antimicrobial*: standard for green tea base; mildly augmented by the jasmine volatile fraction.
- *Mood / olfactory pathway*: the strong aromatic register engages olfactory and limbic pathways; clinical aromatherapy literature on linalool and jasmine supports modest mood-elevating effects via inhalation alone, which carries to ingested jasmine tea's experience.
- *Cross-system convergence*: the Chinese 理氣解鬱 + Ayurvedic _manas-prasādana_ + modern linalool-anxiolytic pharmacology converge on the *mood-elevating* register; this is one of the cleaner alignments in scented-tea pharmacology.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* same as green tea — 3–5 cups/day brewed jasmine tea (~600–1000 mL) for healthy non-pregnant adults; well-tolerated. Caffeine guidance applies (≤200 mg/day in pregnancy). Iron-deficient: separate from iron-rich meals by 1–2 hours. Jasmine-allergy: avoid. Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.

